      "settings": {
        "eyebrow": "Buying guide",
        "heading": "Whole spices from Idukki, and what each one is for",
        "body": "<p>These are the whole spices we pack from farms around Rajakumari and Adimali in Idukki, plus the few we buy in. Pick by the dish.</p><ul><li><strong><a href=\"/products/kerala-cardamom\">Cardamom</a> (elakka):</strong> extra bold 8 to 8.5 mm pods for chai, payasam and biryani.</li><li><strong><a href=\"/products/kerala-black-pepper\">Black pepper</a> (kurumulaku):</strong> bold whole peppercorns for pepper chicken, rasam and everyday grinding.</li><li><strong><a href=\"/products/kerala-white-pepper\">White pepper</a>:</strong> milder, for light-coloured soups and sauces.</li><li><strong><a href=\"/products/kerala-adimali-clove\">Adimali cloves</a> (grampu):</strong> whole buds for biryani, garam masala and tea.</li><li><strong>Cinnamon (karuvappatta):</strong> true <a href=\"/products/aromatic-true-cinnamon-ceylon\">Ceylon cinnamon</a>, imported from Sri Lanka, and <a href=\"/products/premium-cassia-cinnamon\">cassia</a>, for different jobs. Our <a href=\"/blogs/cooking-spices/ceylon-vs-cassia-cinnamon\">Ceylon vs cassia guide</a> explains which to use.</li><li><strong><a href=\"/products/star-anise-chakra-phool-premium-whole-spice\">Star anise</a> (chakra phool):</strong> bought in, for biryani and masala.</li></ul><p>Everything is packed whole with nothing added. Cardamom, pepper and cloves lose their aroma fastest once ground, so they are the best to buy whole from the farm first.</p>",
        "faq_heading": "Questions about our spices"
      }
    },
